The build-verify gate appears three times: at Step 0 (pre-flight),
in the Step 1 loop stop sequence (before git add), and in Step 2
(before --continue). The repetition is intentional -- each occurrence
is at a different commit boundary where rerere caching is the specific
risk, and the stakes differ enough that collapsing them into one
paragraph would obscure which gate applies where.

The in-session pause note is placed only in bulk-catchup-rebase, not
in resolve-merge-conflicts. Single-conflict resolution doesn't carry
the same loop momentum; the note belongs where the risk is highest.

The --rebase-merges subsection deliberately has no alternate loop
body. Encoding a loop before it has been lived through would be
worse than acknowledging the gap and stopping. The reset-to-original-
tip hint is included because it's the non-obvious part; the
--rebase-merges flag itself is easy to find in git docs.
